  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_3677dcc57a1d4a8b92c7ab823f6c9c5d",
      "statement": "Following Knight, risk (outcomes with assignable measurable probabilities) is distinguished from uncertainty proper (no assignable probabilities), and portfolio theory lives in the domain of risk, attaching a probability to each of S future states of the world.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L16-L16",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:573e3613b6cc4ed5e1392fc1dc26b7b11623c0e1023e734b914389f0bba8a0e7:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_85b2ce8c65374cec842c5c98b25b959c",
      "statement": "The expected-utility paradigm assumes a von Neumann-Morgenstern utility that depends only on consequences and is additive across states, so investors maximize U(W) = sum_s pi_s*u(W_s) = E[u(W)].",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L22-L24",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2b9d03c12c6ac67b207e845f02714e9b7fd6182385e7a92a2e09779c5a0d0e0d:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_d8460704fe40486a8a4b9283be013fc3",
      "statement": "Risk aversion corresponds to a wealth-utility function that increases less than proportionally, i.e. concavity (u''(x) < 0), while non-satiation corresponds to u'(x) > 0.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L45-L51",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:56f868f3520a629a77c0dd032c85cc0ac0e02035733b229d3cdf52fbb06b5c20:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_98a0be5a13f34ed99771624209e5cc47",
      "statement": "Under the five axioms (reflexivity, transitivity, completeness, Archimedean, substitution) the vNM theorem guarantees a utility function u with X1 preferred to X2 iff E(u(X1)) >= E(u(X2)), unique up to a positive affine transformation a + b*u(x) with b>0.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L62-L73",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:deb41c35319fdc4d276ac4924520ec09eecca47deebca2d15cfb5e1516f6aa34:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_42e80fd0bfc64f5c813bd17f7247ebca",
      "statement": "The certainty equivalent CE = u^{-1}(E[u(W)]) satisfies, for a concave utility, Jensen's inequality u(CE) = E[u(W)] < u(E[W]), so CE < E[W] and a risk-averse investor prefers the certain E[W] to a lottery of equal expected value.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L79-L83",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e648f35c1cf6ee343cf55ad14ef9bcb12bea4133f0f3b821ed84a8482f5b68e2:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_e287e531564d4f4b8692b63b37aa112e",
      "statement": "The risk premium is defined as E(W) - CE, the expected value an investor will give up to eliminate randomness.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L85-L87",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c5af4db502ff320dde87eb6ac4438006e76439441a3238ec05df1370a3cd732b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_a334402599074b079917f685dd259266",
      "statement": "The Arrow-Pratt measures standardize the second derivative by the first: absolute risk aversion ARA = -u''(W)/u'(W) and relative risk aversion RRA = -u''(W)*W/u'(W).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L91-L94",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:342efb08b6d0d3e06ea7c725ef4521b99f1f4f75376e35f603c2ac5d2b935c24:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_34f76716ff3f4854af9b189d361027b8",
      "statement": "The CARA family u(W) = -e^{-rho*W} has constant ARA = rho, and the CRRA family u(W) = W^{1-gamma}/(1-gamma) (or ln W at gamma=1) has constant RRA = gamma.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L96-L100",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:360081bf94e513492e7aa315868d08a59fd51ec59d03890507629816c4b46e78:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_dff907e8c9ed4c30b4a96bd78be00102",
      "statement": "Quadratic utility u(x) = x - (a/2)x^2 is concave (u''=-a<0) but satisfies non-satiation u'(x)=1-ax>0 only for x < 1/a, so unlike log and exponential utility it is not increasing over the whole positive domain.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L102-L104",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:4f292cf810d076e6fba3b6d0396375d32d76f9fe87da544115a133cb97acb38d:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_2257a6dc18844a3fb3e31b8055c349a1",
      "statement": "Expected utility depends only on mean and variance in three circumstances: approximately for small risks, exactly if returns are normally distributed, or exactly if utility is quadratic.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L108-L113",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:39cd2d9d13ceeeb56699d296e7e720249360adcea9de0555420aa7df1c0d9fa0:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_e1c2e49fc9454140b68e769df554acae",
      "statement": "Quadratic utility has a satiation maximum beyond which investors prefer less to more, and its absolute risk aversion is increasing, ARA = a/(1-a*W), making it a tractable approximation rather than a complete utility specification.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L115-L119",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:11475eb9c18272d7dc21eec8da50a4c09e1c6fa3e65a3589cd0421700f236fd3:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_55ed4c119ddc448e82f736358acedd1a",
      "statement": "Mean-variance dominance is consistent with expected utility only under two mutually exclusive conditions: quadratic utility over portfolio return, or a multivariate elliptical joint return distribution (of which the multivariate normal and Student-t are special cases).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L121-L125",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:fabe8ba8758a595c82c4b79c2d61baf2b9b8530d068be155f28831c6ccb17bdb:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_5c32e725ac9e4a8b990c9dd270478430",
      "statement": "Using a variance-covariance model for non-elliptic distributions can severely underestimate the events that cause the most severe losses.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L125-L125",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:22b1181399d9448bd008622ab2df0c05c58d54d58ce53446d9cfebe49532fc2d:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_c24aabd034244b2b97c8609a999b80d1",
      "statement": "Mean-variance indifference curves E(r) = c + (1/2)A*sigma^2 have slope A*sigma > 0 and second derivative A > 0, so they are increasing and convex, and are steeper for higher A (a more risk-averse investor demands more compensation for the same added risk).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L151-L155",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a18da5849f55048e5a693f5a5d193a8202209b87968008fa0e88e7aa179f164d:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3090f031d17149789747bbe3ced58f66",
      "statement": "With a risk-free asset, maximizing mean-variance utility over the complete portfolio gives the optimal risky share y* = (E(r_T)-r_f)/(A*sigma_T^2), inversely proportional to risk aversion A and variance sigma_T^2 and proportional to the excess return.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/01_choice_under_uncertainty.md#L167-L175",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:db522443fbcea3b0022ff29924cce2fd11f0c2e96f13d74893bbe0b97eb74a4c:"
        }
      ]
    }
